.TH PCAPWHY 1
.SH NAME
pcapwhy \- summarize offline packet capture evidence
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pcapwhy
[--max-packets COUNT] [--max-flows COUNT] [--format json] CAPTURE [CAPTURE ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Reads offline PCAP and PCAPNG files and emits bounded structured evidence about
IPv4 and IPv6 flows, Ethernet MAC addresses, VLAN tags, TCP resets, ICMP and
ICMPv6 control messages, and payload metadata. Multiple captures are compared
by observed directional flow, which is useful for SPAN or TAP captures from
different network locations.
.PP
The command never captures live traffic, transmits packets, changes interfaces,
or emits payload bytes. Capture position, packet loss, encryption, NAT, and
asymmetric routing can limit attribution; a reset or ICMP source is evidence of
what was observed, not proof of the policy device that originated it.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --max-packets COUNT
Maximum packets read from each capture, from 1 through 1000000. The default is
100000. Hitting the bound returns a partial result.
.TP
.B --max-flows COUNT
Maximum directional flows retained per capture, from 1 through 100000. The
default is 10000.
.TP
.B --format json
Emit the versioned JSON report (the only supported format).
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 means complete bounded evidence was collected; 2 invalid usage; 3 a capture
reached the packet bound; and 4 an unreadable or malformed capture/runtime
failure.
